{"slug": "maximum-power-transfer-theorem", "targets": ["maximum power transfer theorem proof"], "html": "<h2 style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:700;color:#0186e0;margin:34px 0 16px;\">Maximum Power Transfer Theorem Proof in Short Steps</h2><p><strong>The maximum power transfer theorem proof</strong> takes a source reduced to its Thevenin equivalent, writes load power as a function of load resistance, and sets the derivative to zero. The result is RL = Rth and Pmax = Vth<sup>2</sup> / 4Rth.</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:16px;\"><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Step</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Working</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Result</th></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1. Load current</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">I = Vth / (Rth + RL)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Current in the series loop</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">2. Load power</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">P = I<sup>2</sup> RL = Vth<sup>2</sup> RL / (Rth + RL)<sup>2</sup></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Power as a function of RL</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">3. Differentiate</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">dP/dRL = Vth<sup>2</sup> [(Rth + RL)<sup>2</sup> minus 2RL(Rth + RL)] / (Rth + RL)<sup>4</sup></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Quotient rule</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">4. Set to zero</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">(Rth + RL) minus 2RL = 0</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">RL = Rth</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">5. Check maximum</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">d<sup>2</sup>P/dRL<sup>2</sup> is negative at RL = Rth</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Point is a maximum</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">6. Maximum power</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">P = Vth<sup>2</sup> Rth / (2Rth)<sup>2</sup></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Pmax = Vth<sup>2</sup> / 4Rth</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">7. Efficiency</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">&eta; = RL / (Rth + RL)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">50 percent at match</td></tr></table></div><h3 style=\"font-size:19px;font-weight:700;color:#1a2b4a;margin:20px 0 10px;\">Quick Check With Numbers</h3><p>For Vth = 12 V and Rth = 4 &Omega;, the best load is 4 &Omega;. Pmax = 144 / 16 = 9 W. Try RL = 2 &Omega;: I = 2 A and P = 8 W. Try RL = 8 &Omega;: I = 1 A and P = 8 W. Both values are below 9 W, which confirms the proof.</p><p>For AC circuits the same method applied to both resistance and reactance gives ZL equal to the complex conjugate of Zth.</p>"}